title: A flip-flop that flips
mode: draw
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
parts: dff not
---- intro
A flip-flop's output can go back into its own input. Put a NOT gate on the way and something new happens: at every clock tick the flip-flop copies the **opposite** of what it holds, so its output flips, 0, 1, 0, 1.

Feeding an output back like this is called **feedback**. Without a flip-flop in the loop, a NOT feeding itself would never settle; with one, the loop only moves one step per tick.

Press **Step** to tick the clock once, or **Run** to keep it going.
---- task
Make the light `q` flip at every tick of `clk`, using one flip-flop and one NOT gate.
---- start
clock clk 0 80
led q 400 20
---- reference
clock clk
led q
wire t[1]
t = dff(~t, clk)
q = t
---- answer
clock clk 0 80
dff f1 160 20
not n1 160 120
led q 400 20
clk -> f1.clk
f1.q -> n1.in
n1 -> f1.d
f1.q -> q
---- hint1
The flip-flop's `q` goes to the light, and also into a NOT gate. Where does the NOT gate's output go?
---- hint2
Wire the NOT gate's output back into the flip-flop's `d` input, and `clk` into its `clk`.
---- solution
`q` goes to the light and into the NOT gate, and the NOT gate goes back into `d`. Each tick, the flip-flop takes the opposite of what it held. The light flips at every tick.
---- done
The light flips once per tick: it goes on and off half as often as the clock does. That makes this circuit a **divide by two**, and it is the first step of a counter.
